# Tangerine Compiler — x86-64 (System V AMD64) LIR backend
# Self-hosting compiler written in Tangerine
#
# AUDIT ITEM 30, STAGE 2 — THE CONSTRUCT SLICE. This module is an x86-64
# code generator over the SAME machine IR (LIR) every other backend
# consumes: it takes a LirFunction + its LirAlloc (already verified by
# verify_lir against the module's LirVerifyX86_64 route) and emits x86-64
# machine code into the shared CodeBuffer, then serializes the whole
# program (text, .data/.bss and RELA relocations) as an ELF64 x86-64
# relocatable object (e_machine 62, EM_X86_64). It is NOT a second
# direct-emission path: every statement and terminator arm below mirrors
# the aarch64 emitter's semantics (lir.tg's lir_emit_fn family) and the
# RISC-V backend's structure (backend_rv.tg — its emit-state /
# def-target / use / prologue / patch-resolution shape, def-for-def with
# x86_64 names).
#
# Stage 2 adds, with real emission (never a silently weaker path):
#   - AGGREGATES/ENUMS/SRET: the LIR word-group model (aggregate word
#     vregs homed at fixed frame slots) with whole-word loads/stores and
#     the caller/callee aggregate ABI under the SysV registers: an
#     address-class aggregate parameter/argument crosses as its frame
#     address in ONE general slot (the LIR-internal image ABI, exactly
#     the aarch64 pair/memory rule), a Scalar-class one-word image
#     crosses by value in its slot; sret returns use the SysV hidden
#     pointer (rdi, saved at entry and returned in rax) with the
#     argument registers shifted down at entry and up at the call site;
#   - FLOATS (SSE2): XMM0..7 for the fixed float ABI ids 8..15, the
#     allocatable pool xmm8..14 (phys 108..114), the statement scratch
#     xmm15; movss/movsd, addsd/subsd/mulsd/divsd (and ss), comisd/
#     ucomisd with an ordered/unordered decode for LirFCmp+LirCset,
#     cvtsi2sd/cvttsd2si with the §25 SATURATING fixup and the unsigned
#     int->float fixup, F32<->F64 CVT, MOVD/MOVQ class crossings;
#   - STATICS: .data PROGBITS / .bss NOBITS with GLOBAL STT_OBJECT
#     symbols and RIP-relative R_X86_64_PC32 (kind 2, addend -4)
#     static-value loads;
#   - VECTORS v128 (SSE2): the packed add/sub/and/or/xor families over
#     the lane-appropriate opcodes, MOVDQU load/store, and PSHUFB
#     shuffle when (and only when) the target description advertises
#     the ssse3 token — the baseline x86_64 desc does not, so the
#     shuffle arm fails closed with the named SSSE3 rule; the packed
#     multiply covers the 16-bit lane class (PMULLW), the 32-bit lane
#     classes (the full-lane multiply is the SSE2 PMULUDQ-twice +
#     pshufd/punpckldq sequence, upgraded to the single PMULLD when the
#     desc advertises the sse41 token) and f32/f64 (MULPS/MULPD); the
#     byte and qword packed multiplies fail closed with the named ISA
#     rule (no PMULLB exists through AVX2; VPMULLQ is AVX-512DQ), never
#     a scalar fallback;
#   - POINTER/ATOMICS: the plain deref forms (LirPtrLea/Addr/Load/Store
#     at the authority's 1/2/4/8 widths with the signed/unsigned load
#     split) and the x86-TSO atomic forms (plain MOV loads and Relaxed
#     stores, the implicitly locked XCHG for the Release/SeqCst store
#     form, LOCK XADD for the fetch-add family, LOCK CMPXCHG retry
#     loops for the and/or/xor families and for CAS, XCHG for exchange,
#     MFENCE for the form-1 fence). Every atomic width is validated
#     against THE ONE ATOMIC AUTHORITY over x86_64_desc (native
#     1/2/4/8, no emulated class).
#
# ALL-NATIVE: this backend never invokes clang/llvm or any external
# toolchain, in code or in the pipeline. External tools were used ONLY as
# offline verification aids for the hand-derived byte layouts: every
# encoding's bytes below were cross-checked against the output of
# `clang --target=x86_64` + `objdump -d` (a subset of the verified
# patterns is pinned by tests/x86_64_lir_rows_test.tg and every encoder
# carries its layout + a hand-verified byte pattern in its comment). The
# encoders return the raw instruction BYTES (x86-64 is variable-length —
# the one structural difference from backend_rv.tg's single-word rv_*
# encoders) and the emitter appends them through x86_emit; every encoder
# fails closed with a panic on an out-of-range displacement, never a
# silently truncated field.
#
# REGISTER MODEL (SysV AMD64 — target_desc.tg's x86_64_desc arg_regs):
#   fixed LIR general vregs 0..5 -> rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx, r8, r9 (the six
#     SysV integer argument registers). Fixed ids 6 and 7 have NO SysV
#     integer register (SysV passes arguments 7+ on the stack): the
#     route FAILS CLOSED on any function/statement that uses fixed ids
#     6/7 or the stack stream (LirPushArg/LirLoadFrame; see
#     x86_validate_signature/x86_validate_fn).
#   fixed LIR float vregs 8..15 -> xmm0..xmm7 (the SysV SSE argument
#     registers and the return register xmm0 = fixed id 8).
#   allocator general pool ids 9..14 -> r10, r11, rbx, r12, r13, r14
#     (x86_phys_of). The allocator treats them as caller-saved: a
#     register-resident value crossing a call is stored to its slot
#     before the call and reloaded at its first read after it
#     (LirAlloc.spills/reloads). rbx/r12/r13/r14 are CALLEE-saved in the
#     SysV ABI, so the prologue saves every one of this pool the
#     allocation actually assigned (x86_saved_regs scans the segment
#     records) — the allocator's caller-saved bookkeeping handles call
#     crossings, the prologue handles the ABI preservation.
#   allocator FP pool phys 108..114 -> xmm8..xmm14 (x86_fp_pool_ids).
#     All SysV XMM registers are caller-saved, so no FP save/restore
#     exists; xmm15 is the FP statement scratch (def temp, spill-load
#     temp and the FNeg/FConst/F32-rounding scratch) and xmm0..xmm7 are
#     the fixed ABI file — neither is ever allocated.
#   allocator callee-saved id 19 -> r15 (the one SysV callee-saved
#     register left after the frame pointer and the pool above). The
#     route passes cc_pool = [19] to lir_alloc_registers_mode, so a
#     whole call-crossing hull may ride r15 with no spill record;
#     alloc.cc_used names it and the prologue saves/restores it.
#   rax = the statement TEMP and the return register. It is never
#     allocated to a vreg. RSP/RBP are the frame machinery. RDX/RCX/RSI/
#     RDI are additional short-lived scratch at the statements that need
#     them (FP compares, conversion fixups, atomic CAS loops, the FNeg
#     mask park): the fixed general/float vregs appear only in entry
#     binds and pre-call argument moves, and no such statement exists in
#     those two sequences, so the ABI file is dead at every scratch use.
#   The frame is the aarch64/LIR uniform model: spill slot i at
#   [rbp - 8 - 8*i] (lir_slot_offset), plus ONE emitter scratch slot at
#   index alloc.slots ([rbp - 8 - 8*alloc.slots]) for the rare forms
#   that must park a 64-bit immediate, the divisor, a switch operand or
#   the unsigned-conversion temporary, plus the callee-saved save area
#   at the bottom of the frame.
#
# STILL FAIL-CLOSED (each construct names its rule in
# x86_validate_fn/x86_emit_stmt; verify_lir's X86_64 route is the
# mandatory second gate):
#   - the SysV caller/callee STACK-argument stream (LirPushArg /
#     LirLoadFrame / call stack_args): the lowering's stack geometry is
#     the aarch64 16-byte caller slot (16 + 16*k) and the SysV stream is
#     8-byte; a general-class parameter/argument beyond r9 (six), a
#     float beyond xmm7 (eight), and an sret call with more than five
#     general arguments all fail closed;
#   - fixed general vregs 6/7 (arguments 7..8);
#   - vector shuffle without the ssse3 token on v128 / without the
#     avx2 token on v256 (pshufb / vpshufb; the baseline x86_64 desc is
#     SSE2-only — a runtime per-byte select has no SSE2 encoding) and
#     the no-SIMD-class packed multiplies (i8/i64 — no PMULLB/VPMULLQ
#     exists through AVX2; the 32-bit lane multiply never fails closed:
#     it uses the SSE4.1 PMULLD under the desc's sse41 token and the
#     SSE2 PMULUDQ sequence otherwise);
#   - v256 without the desc's avx token (the ymm envelope: loads/stores,
#     the FP/logical VEX forms and every vector ABI crossing) and the
#     256-bit INTEGER VEX forms (VPADD/VPADDQ/VPADDD/VPSUB*/VPMULLW/
#     VPMULLD ymm) without the avx2 token — every gate names the
#     descriptor;
#   - the shared shape gates: Char/
#     I64/U64/I128/U128/string, tuple/array/@packed/@align/zst aggregate
#     layouts, sub-word fields sharing or straddling a word, and
#     payloaded-enum crossings to extern symbols
#     (lir_check_extern_enum_calls);
#   - MirDeinit/drop glue (NO LONGER fail-closed on this route —
#     WIRED for the whole-program route): the
#     x86 lowering entry receives the same program deinit/finalizer/
#     glue tables the aarch64 route carries (MirProgram.deinit_plans /
#     method_names_by_def / drop_glue_fns, built in
#     x86_64_compile_object), so structural cleanup, user finalizers,
#     the String destructor and the generated drop-glue bodies lower
#     here exactly as on aarch64 (only the instruction emission
#     differs; the old fail-closed drop rule is gone on this route);
#   - extern calls DO lower: `call rel32` plus an R_X86_64_PLT32
#     relocation (kind 4, addend -4) against the undefined symbol, so
#     the linker resolves them. Intra-object calls to defined program
#     functions are deferred rel32 patches resolved once every function
#     label is in the shared buffer.
#   DIVISION is in scope: the marked LirBinop emits cqo+idiv (signed) /
#   xor edx,edx+div (unsigned) with the quotient in rax, behind the
#   language-edge guard (audit item 30): divisor zero and the signed
#   INT64_MIN/-1 pair ABORT through a call to the runtime's whitelisted
#   __intrinsic_abort symbol on both targets/routes (never the aarch64
#   architectural 0/MIN, never an inline ud2 in a user function).
#   @cold is ADVISORY and NOT specialized here: every body is emitted in
#   program order into one .text (the hint changes nothing).

use std::core::{Option, Result, panic}
use std::collections::{Vec, Set}
use tg_compiler::mir::{MirProgram, MirFunction, MirStatic, MirTypeDef}
use tg_compiler::types::{DeinitPlan}
use tg_compiler::lir::{
  LirFunction, LirAlloc, LirStmt, LirStmtKind, LirTermKind, LirValue,
